meta {
  name: Get Public Lead Form
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/public/lead-form/{{shareLinkToken}}
  body: none
  auth: none
}

tests {
  test("returns 200 OK without any Authorization header", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(200);
  });

  test("exposes EXACTLY agencyName and isActive — nothing else", function () {
    const keys = Object.keys(res.getBody()).sort();
    expect(keys).to.eql(["agencyName", "isActive"]);
  });

  test("leaks no producer identity or internal ids", function () {
    const raw = JSON.stringify(res.getBody());
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("producerId");
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("agencyId");
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("branchId");
    expect(raw).to.not.contain("submissionCount");
  });
}

docs {
  # GET /public/lead-form/:token — render data for the public form (PAC-37)

  **Public** (`@Public()`): no JWT, no `AccessContext`, and every one of the six
  global guards is bypassed. Run **Create Share Link** first — it captures
  `shareLinkToken`.

  Returns only what is needed to render an agency-branded form to an outsider.

  ## Response `200`
  ```json
  { "agencyName": "Smith Family Agency", "isActive": true }
  ```

  That is the **complete** payload. No producer name or id, no `agencyId` /
  `branchId`, no link id, no submission count, no client data — and no lead
  source, because a share-link lead deliberately has none. Every field added
  here becomes visible to anyone on the internet holding the URL, so the e2e
  suite asserts the key list by deep equality rather than spot-checking.

  ## Tenancy without a guard chain
  Because `@Public()` skips `TenantGuard` and `ModuleGuard`, the service resolves
  everything from the **share-link record itself** and re-checks by hand:
  the link exists and is active, the agency exists and is `active`, the `leads`
  module is enabled for it, and the link's producer is still an active user.
  Nothing is ever taken from the request body or query string.

  ## Rate limiting
  `10 requests / minute / IP` by default (`PUBLIC_FORM_RATE_LIMIT`).
  Deliberately **looser** than the submit limit: this returns only an agency
  name, and someone completing a form on a phone will reload the page. Locking
  them out would cost a lead and protect nothing.

  The client IP is taken from the first `X-Forwarded-For` entry, so the limit is
  per real caller rather than per load balancer.

  ## Errors
  - `404 Not Found` — `{"message": "This form is no longer available.", ...}`.
    Returned **identically** for an unknown token, a revoked link, a malformed
    token, an inactive agency, a disabled `leads` module, and a deactivated
    producer. The response cannot be used to learn whether a token ever existed.
  - `429 Too Many Requests` — rate limit exceeded.
}
